==============================================================================
[D] GATE B CLAUSE B4 — executed as written, and the clause is DEFECTIVE
==============================================================================
  B4 as pre-registered: 'Any argument whose mechanism bounds n, or whose conclusion
  has the form `n >= ...` / "the class is finite", is REFUSED without being read.'
  Corollary D' concludes literally  n >= 1 + a(v).  So B4 FIRES, as written.
  PASS  B4 fires on Corollary D' by its literal text   [conclusion form 'n >= ...']

  BUT the clause is mis-drafted, and the owner drafted it.  B4 exists because G49
  (draft 28.2) makes n UNBOUNDED, killing routes that derive a contradiction from
  bounding n ABOVE.  A LOWER bound n >= f(local data) does not bound n, is consistent
  with G49, and cannot produce the contradiction B4 was built to refuse.  The clause
  lists a lower-bound FORM as an instance of 'bounds n', which is a drafting error.

  HANDLING: the refusal STANDS as written -- the gate is executed, not reinterpreted
  mid-harvest -- so Corollary D' is NOT adopted this round.  This costs nothing
  concrete (B1/B2 adopt nothing on a single engine return anyway).  The clause is
  referred to the planner for repair.  A gate silently reinterpreted when it is
  inconvenient is not a pre-registered gate.

